      "Language": "eng",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": "Two independent clinical studies were conducted to compare the gastrointestinal (GI) tolerability of corn starch fiber, a novel dietary fiber, at up to 50\u2009g/day (single-dose study) or 90\u2009g/day (multiple-serving study) with a negative control (no fiber) and a positive control (50 or 90\u2009g polydextrose, for single- and multiple-serving studies, respectively) in generally healthy study volunteers. Flatulence and borborygmus were the primary symptoms reported at the higher doses of corn starch fiber and for the positive control interventions. Bowel movements were increased over 48\u2009h with corn starch fiber at 90\u2009g. Thresholds for mild GI effects were established at 30\u2009g as a single dose and 60\u2009g as multiple servings spread over the day. Other than moderate abdominal pain and mild increased appetite in one subject at 90-g corn starch fiber, no test article-related adverse events were reported."},
